5841064453125, 0.007007598876953125, 0.01308441162109375, 0.0191192626953125, -0.0274658203125, -0.011016845703125, 0.0258636474609375, 0.0026226043701171875, 0.0215606689453125, -0.0240478515625, 0.033935546875, 0.049835205078125, 0.0226593017578125, 0.00992584228515625, 0.005985260009765625, -0.0124359130859375, 0.0275726318359375, 0.04302978515625, -0.04931640625, 0.005451202392578125, -0.061553955078125, -0.05352783203125, 0.00897979736328125, -0.007480621337890625, -0.005962371826171875, -0.0145263671875, -0.000774383544921875, -0.0099639892578125, 0.01538848876953125, -0.0034008026123046875, -0.0191650390625, -0.00955963134765625, 0.00856781005859375, 0.0051422119140625, 0.0129852294921875, -0.0011768341064453125, -0.0009908676147460938, -0.004638671875, 0.0160064697265625, 0.0042724609375, -0.0030384063720703125, 0.0325927734375, 0.00897979736328125, 0.01395416259765625, -0.0108795166015625, 0.0007386207580566406, 0.01522064208984375, -0.006744384765625, 0.002086639404296875, -0.003559112548828125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Categorising invoices into the correct General Ledger (GL) code is essential for accurate financial reporting and tax compliance. This task requires skilled accounting judgement, as the appropriate category depends on nuanced factors such as the purchasing business\u2019s context, the vendor, and the invoice text. While AI is increasingly used to automate such tasks, including invoice categorisation, in-house small language models (SLMs) offer potential advantages in cost, data security, confidentiality, and interpretability. \n\n**Objective and Novelty:**  \nThis study aims to evaluate the effectiveness of in-house SLMs for invoice categorisation, focusing on the relationship between pre-trained embedding geometry and classification performance. Our main contribution is extending prior token-level findings on embedding geometry to sentence embeddings in a financial context, and demonstrating that SLMs can generalise well to new clients and challenging categories with limited data.\n\n**Background and Definitions:**  \nWe analyse the embedding geometry of a small sentence transformer (SBERT) and a classic SLM (DeBERTa). In this context, \"anisotropic\" refers to the embedding space being unevenly distributed globally, while \"isotropic clusters\" are locally uniform groupings within this space. These clusters are found to be strongly correlated with vendor identity, suggesting that vendor information is a key signal in the data.\n\n**Methods:**  \nOur dataset consists of approximately 10,000 invoices from multiple clients, each labelled with a GL code and vendor identity. Preprocessing involved anonymising sensitive information, standardising text, and tokenising invoice descriptions. We fine-tuned SBERT on a single GPU, using 80% of the data for training and 20% for testing. Baseline comparisons include a zero-shot large language model (LLM) and a vendor identity classifier.\n\n**Results:**  \nSBERT fine-tuned on our dataset achieves 0.96 accuracy on invoice classification, outperforming the zero-shot LLM (0.85 accuracy) and the vendor identity baseline (0.88 accuracy). For smaller, challenging categories and new clients, SBERT achieves an F1 score of 0.90 with roughly 100 client-specific invoices, indicating strong generalisation. We consistently report accuracy for overall classification and F1 score for performance on smaller categories.\n\n**Structured Input Analysis:**  \nWe tested whether providing structured input\u2014such as separating invoice fields or adding explicit labels\u2014would improve SLM performance. Contrary to expectations, this did not yield better results, suggesting that the SLM leverages contextual information from unstructured text more effectively in this setting.\n\n**Discussion and Limitations:**  \nOur findings indicate that in-house SLMs are promising for invoice categorisation, especially when data privacy and interpretability are priorities. However, our study is limited by the size and diversity of the dataset, and results may vary with different invoice formats or industries. Future work should explore larger and more varied datasets, alternative model architectures, and deeper analysis of embedding geometry\u2019s impact on classification.\n\n**Conclusion:**  \nBy combining geometric analysis with classification experiments, we show that pre-trained embedding geometry is associated with classification performance. Our results also reveal that structured input, while helpful for human readers, does not necessarily benefit SLMs in this context. This work highlights the potential and challenges of deploying in-house SLMs for financial document categorisation."}}